  "_about": "WHAT AUTO-TAGGING LOOKS FOR in a scene's action lines (PRD 2.0 FR-603). Read by src/lib/screenplay-analysis.js `suggestElements()`. Content, not code, for invariant 2.\n\nCAST is not in this file: a character is whoever has a CUE in the scene, plus anybody introduced in capitals in the action (\"RAVI, 30s, counts coins\") — the screenplay convention that a speaking part is capitalised on first appearance. That needs no word list.\n\nEXTRAS, SET DRESSING, WARDROBE, MAKEUP, ANIMALS, SFX, VFX and SPECIAL are added the same way: lower-case whole words. They are looser than props, because a department reads every suggestion against the line it came from before accepting it, and a missed element on a breakdown costs more than an extra chip. \n\nSOUND also reads the convention: a sound effect written in capitals in action (\"the phone RINGS\", \"a GUNSHOT\") is a cue, which is how a sound department breaks a script down. The words below catch the ones written in lower case.\n\nEvery entry is a lower-case whole word or phrase. Matching is whole-word, so `car` does not fire on `scarf` and `bus` does not fire on `business`. Tamil-production vocabulary (auto, lorry, two-wheeler, nadaswaram) is listed alongside English because that is how Chennai scripts are written.\n\nA SUGGESTION IS NOT A TAG. The breakdown shows every suggestion with the line it came from and adds only what somebody accepts; nothing here writes to a scene by itself.",
